# OpenPronounce API — Production Development SPEC v1.0

> **Status:** Ready for implementation  
> **Date:** 2026-11-20  
> **Project:** `openpronounce-api`  
> **Scope:** English word, phrase and sentence reading assessment; CPU-only; upload + URL input; Docker / GitHub Actions / GHCR.

## 1. Goal and decisions

Build an independent, maintainable **FastAPI service** wrapping the OpenPronounce Python library. It accepts a target English text and a learner's recorded audio, evaluates pronunciation on CPU, and returns a stable, application-friendly JSON contract. The service is reusable by .NET/Blazor, mobile, and automation clients.

### Locked technology decisions

| Item | Decision |
|---|---|
| Runtime | **Python 3.12** (`>=3.12,<3.13`) |
| API | FastAPI + Pydantic v2 + Uvicorn |
| Scoring engine | `openpronounce==1.1.0` initially; pin transitive dependencies with a lockfile |
| Device | CPU only: `OPENPRONOUNCE_DEVICE=cpu` |
| Assessment | Known English text: word / phrase / sentence; no free speech endpoint in v1 |
| TTS / generated reference | **Disabled** (`acoustic=False`); no Kokoro, Piper, Google TTS |
| Recognition | `transcription=True` by default to retain wrong/missing-word feedback |
| Prosody contour generation | `prosody=False` by default (does **not** disable the engine's trained `prosodic` score) |
| Input | Multipart audio upload **and** HTTPS audio URL |
| Audio decoding | WAV / MP3 / FLAC / OGG / WebM, through supported decoder stack (`ffmpeg` / `load_audio`) |
| Distribution | Docker Compose + image pushed to GitHub Container Registry (GHCR) |
| Tests | Pytest unit/API + security cases; optional real-model integration and Docker smoke tests |
| Database / UI | None in v1 |

### Explicit non-goals

No TTS playback, speech cloning, open-ended ASR chat, account management, persistent audio storage, scheduled processing, queue/database, model training, automatic pronunciation coaching with an LLM, or arbitrary remote URL fetching without egress controls.

### Upstream assumptions to verify at implementation

- `openpronounce.assess(waveform, text, lang="en", acoustic=False, prosody=False, transcription=True)` delegates to the read-speech engine in 1.1.0.
- `load_audio(path)` normalizes accepted source audio to a 16 kHz mono waveform.
- The trained scores are separate from the older synthetic-audio `formula_score`. With `acoustic=False`, `formula_score` / `acoustic_distance` may be unavailable; this is expected.
- `reliable=false` means the score is unsuitable for user-facing pass/fail; map it to an uncertain result.
- Upstream **evaluated sentence readings**, not a guaranteed single-word correctness classifier. Unit tests must not invent quantitative model accuracy.

Documentation: https://openpronounce.github.io/docs/read-speech.html and https://openpronounce.github.io/docs/results.html

